der, tabs, then form: photo upload left (dashed zone) + fields right. Two columns of fields: First name / Last name, Email / Role. Photo upload zone as a card.\n\n**06_Messages**:\n- Left: conversation list panel (~380 wide): \"Messages\" header, search \"Search...\" with Icon_Search, \"PINNED\" section with 3 conversations (Killan James 4:30 PM Typing... unread 2; Design Team 9:36 AM Hello! Everyone; Ahmed Medi 1:15 AM Wow really Cool 🔥), \"All Messages\" section with 5 (Claudia Maudi 4:30 PM Typing...; Novita 4:30 PM yah, nice design · 2 unread; Milie Nose 8:20 PM Awesome 🔥 · 1; Ikhsan SD yesterday · Voice message; Aditya yesterday · publish now). Current: Design Team (highlighted).\n- Middle: conversation with Design Team: header (Avatar_Design_Team, \"Design Team\", \"60 members, 10 online\", member avatars, Icon_Plus), messages per data with day divider \"— Today, March 24 —\", composer \"Add a comment...\" with Icon_Mic, Icon_Emoji, Icon_Image, Icon_Attachment, Icon_Send.\n- Right: contact panel (~300): Killan James avatar + name + @killanjames? Wait — contact panel shows Killan James? The brief says: \"Contact panel: Avatar_Killan_James · 'Killan James' · '@killanjames' · 'Attachments' 4 files (Icon_File) with size and date · 'View all' · 'Members' · '+ Add Member' · Novita · Milie Nose · Ikhsan SD · Aditya with avatars\". Interesting — the contact panel shows the pinned contact Killan James even though open conversation is Design Team. OK, follow the brief exactly.\n  - Attachments: Very important file.figma, Some file.scratch, List of something.xd, Very important file.svg — each 7.5 MB · 3.22.22, 11:15 AM. \"View all\".\n  - Members: \"+ Add Member\", then Novita, Milie Nose, Ikhsan SD, Aditya with avatars.\n\nConversation messages:\n- Killan James · 10:12 AM: \"Hi, are you still a web designer?\" / \"Would love to see some designs 😁\" (two bubbles)\n- Day divider: \"Today, March 24\"\n- Claudia Maudi · 10:30 AM: \"Hey, happy to hear from you. Yes, I will be back in a couple of days.\" / \"Here are some designs I took earlier today.\" + [Photo_Chat_Design_1, Photo_Chat_Design_2]\n- Dristin Watson (you) · 10:30 AM: \"Great 🔥 That's a nice design idea. 😍👏\" (right-aligned, brand bubble)\n- Milie Nose · 8:20 PM: \"Awesome 🔥\"\n\n**Prototype links**:\n- All 6 frames: sidebar icons → corresponding frames (Dashboard icon → 01_Dashboard, Timeline → 02, Task → 03, Settings → 05, Messages → 06, Files → 04). 6 icons × 6 frames = 36 connections (excluding self-links maybe; I can link all anyway — self link harmless but let me skip self).\n- 01_Dashboard: message row(s) → 06_Messages (link the Messages card header and/or message rows).\n\nNow implementation strategy with use_figma:\n\nBecause everything is in ONE file, I can create instances of reference components (avatars, icons, logo, photos, Button, Text Field) directly in the workspace page. That's ideal for \"reuse components\".\n\nI need to be careful about scale: 6 full screens is a LOT of nodes. I'll build with a shared set of helper functions in each use_figma call (the code runs in one evaluation; state doesn't persist between calls, so each call must be self-contained; I'll pass IDs between calls).\n\nPlan of use_figma calls:\n1. Setup: rename page 8:2 to \"03 · Workspace\" (it's already named that). Create page frame structure: I'll build a \"Design System\" section on workspace page? The brief says \"Design all other states ... as reusable components in your file.\" So I should create components: Sidebar (with variants per current item?), TopBar, stat cards, etc. Creating full component variants for everything is heavy. Practical approach: create a small set of core components (Sidebar nav item with current/inactive states, Tag pill variants, Avatar stack, Icon button, Chat bubble variants, Column header, Task card...) — but time/token budget is limited. The evaluation mentions \"Component reuse\". \n\nCompromise: Build the 6 screens using instances of reference components + a handful of my own components created on the workspace page:\n- `Nav Rail` component (full sidebar with logo + 6 icons; parameterize current item via variants? Variants need component props — I can create 6 variants of nav rail, one per screen, each an instance in the screen. That's efficient: build once, instance 6 times with different current states.)\n- `Top Bar` component (same on all screens — build once, instance 6×).\n- Tag pill component with variants (Design, Research, Planning, Content).\n- Icon Button component (ghost square).\n- Maybe stat card, but they have unique content (icon, number, label, sparkline, change) — could be component with overrides. I'll make Stat Card component with text overrides via instances (text override by instance — set characters on instance children). That works.\n\nGiven complexity, I'll build screens largely as authored frames but use components for: Top Bar, Nav Rail (6 variants), Tag, Icon Button, chat bubble? Chat bubbles differ (incoming/outgoing) — could do two components.\n\nActually, let me be pragmatic: The eval criteria include component reuse but coverage/data/flow are first. I'll create these reusable components:\n1. `Top Bar` (instance on all 6)\n2. `Nav Rail` with 6 variants (current = Dashboard/Timeline/Task/Settings/Messages/Files)\n3. `Tag` with 4 variants\n4. `Icon Button` (ghost 40×40 with icon slot → use component with instance swap? simpler: component containing a specific icon; I'll make a generic \"Icon Button\" and override the icon child by swapping instance... Instance swap requires matching component. I can set `instance.swapComponent(iconComp)` on the nested icon instance.)\n5. `Avatar Stack`? The stacks vary in members/count; I'll make a helper function instead that returns a frame of avatar instances.\n6. `Primary Button`/`Secondary Button` (based on given Button component style: primary = given Button instance; secondary = my outline variant).\n\nPlus screens use Text Field instances with overridden label/placeholder/width.\n\nNode count estimate per screen: Dashboard ~ 150, Timeline ~ 150, Board ~ 200, Files ~ 150, Settings ~ 80, Messages ~ 180. Plus components ~ 100. Total ~ 1000+ nodes. Each use_figma call can create maybe 40-80 nodes comfortably within 50k char code limit. So ~ 15-25 calls. That's fine.\n\nStrategy for fonts: Load DM Sans styles: Regular, Medium, Bold (+ maybe SemiBold? DM Sans in Google Fonts has Regular, Medium, Bold; the file uses Medium and Regular per inspection. I'll load: Regular, Medium, Bold. Also need \"Semi Bold\"? Use \"Bold\" for headings. Let me check what styles exist: figma.listAvailableFontsAsync filtered. I'll load Regular/Medium/Bold — standard for DM Sans.\n\nWait — the fontName observed: {family: \"DM Sans\", style: \"Medium\", variationSettings: {wght: 500, opsz: 14}}. This is variable font. figma.loadFontAsync({family:\"DM Sans\", style:\"Medium\"}) should work. Also \"Regular\", \"Bold\". Possibly \"SemiBold\"? For safety I'll try loading Regular, Medium, Bold, and if Bold fails, fallback. I'll verify early.\n\nColor definitions (final):\n- brand/primary: #5051F9\n- brand/hover: #4042D9\n- brand/soft: #EDEDFE (indigo tint bg)\n- accent/blue: #1DA7FF (logo second color, chart series 2)\n- text/primary: #14142B\n- text/secondary: #5B5B6B → let me use #6E7191 (hmm, keep close to placeholder #8C8C9E). Define:\n  - text/primary: #14142B\n  - text/secondary: #61618A? Simplify: secondary #6E7191, muted #8C8C9E, faint #A6A8B8\n- bg/app: #F6F6F9? Given input stroke #E1E2E9 and label #232360, the kit feels cool gray. bg/app: #F4F5F9; surface: #FFFFFF; border: #E8E9F0 / input stroke #E1E2E9.\n- success: #2BB673, success-soft #E3F6ED\n- warning: #FF9F38, warning-soft #FFF2E2\n- danger: #EB5757, danger-soft #FDEAEA\n- chart-2: #D6D6FB (light indigo) or blue #1DA7FF for second series. I'll use brand for series1, #D9D9FA for series2? Actually \"two series\" — series A brand #5051F9, series B #1DA7FF (logo blue) at maybe 60%? Keep both solid.\n- Tag colors: Design (indigo #5051F9 / bg #EDEDFE), Research (teal #12A594 / bg #E0F5F2), Planning (orange #F79009 / bg #FEF0E0), Content (pink #E548A0? or red #EB5757 / bg #FDECF2). Use: Content → #EB5757/#FDEAEA.\n\nType scale:\n- 24 Bold (page titles) line 30\n- 20 Bold (card titles) line 26 — maybe 18\n- 16 SemiBold-ish Medium for section headers → use Medium\n- 14 Regular/Medium body\n- 13 Medium small\n- 12 Regular captions\n- 11 Medium tiny (badges)\n\nSpacing: page padding 24-32; card padding 20-24; gaps 16/24.\n\nRadii: cards 16; inner elements 10; inputs 8; pills full.\n\nShadows: card: 0 1 2 rgba(20,20,43,0.04), 0 8 24 rgba(20,20,43,0.06). Sidebar/topbar: none or subtle.\n\nNow the build order:\n1. Verify workspace page + fonts. Create \"components\" area on workspace page (left of screens, x offset -600?). Better: place screens in a row starting x=0 y=0 with 200 gap; components section below at y=1400. Actually screens: 6 × (1440+160). y=0. Components at y=1300.\n\nHmm wait, should screens be arranged 3×2? Fine either way. I'll do one row.\n\n2. Build components (Top Bar, Nav Rail variants, Tag variants, Icon Button, Chat Bubble in/out, Column? etc.)\n3. Build screens one by one.\n4. Prototype links.\n5. Screenshots + fixes.\n\nOne concern: Nav Rail as component with 6 variants: In each screen, an instance of the right variant. Each variant = frame with logo + 6 icon buttons, current one styled. The icons inside are instances of the reference icon components. Component within component: when creating the Nav Rail component, I insert instances of Icon_Nav_* components — allowed.\n\nPrototype linking from instances: interactions can be set on nodes inside component instances? Prototype connections on nested instance children — actually in Figma you can add interactions to nested content of an instance (it becomes an override in the prototype). Via Plugin API: `node.interpolate... no: `figma.createPage... The API: `node.prototypeInteractions`? There's `prototypeInteractions` property? Hmm. Actually the Plugin API has `node.reactions` (ReactionsProperty) — you set `node.reactions = [{ action: {type: \"NODE\", destinationId}, trigger: {type: \"CLICK\"} }]`? Let me recall: In the Figma Plugin API, interactions are stored as `reactions` on nodes: `node.reactions: Array<Reaction>` where Reaction = { action: Action, trigger: Trigger, ... }. Yes, `reactions` is writable on nodes. For nodes inside instances, reactions can be set on the instance's children? I believe prototype interactions on instance children are supported (that's how component interactions work — actually interactions set on a component's children apply to all instances). Setting reactions on nodes inside a COMPONENT applies everywhere it's instanced.\n\nSo better: put the click interactions ON the Nav Rail component's icon nodes — but each icon must navigate to a different frame depending on which screen the instance is in! Can't do per-instance destinations via component reactions... Actually you CAN override interactions per instance in the prototype UI, but via API, setting reactions on instance children — hmm, I think `reactions` on instance sublayers can be set (it's an override stored on the instance). Let me just try: for each screen, get the nav rail instance, find its icon child instances, set reactions on those children. If that fails, fallback: set reactions on an invisible overlay rectangle in each screen placed above the rail? That's ugly.\n\nAlternative simpler approach: Don't make the nav rail a component; author it per-screen with icon instances and set reactions on the icon instances directly (top-level instance children CAN have reactions set? Actually reactions are typically set on any scene node — including instances and their children? The Plugin API docs: \"reactions: Array<Reaction>\" — available on SceneNode. For children of instances, setting reactions might throw \"Cannot set property on read-only\"... I recall that overrides on instance children include fills, text, etc., and interactions ARE overridable in prototype mode. Via API I believe you can set reactions on instance children. I'll test on first screen.\n\nSafest fallback: make nav rail a component with 6 variants, and set reactions on the ICON nodes INSIDE the component variants — with each icon linking to its target frame. Since the destination frame is the same regardless of which screen the instance lives in (Dashboard icon always → 01_Dashboard), component-level reactions work perfectly! The only issue: on 01_Dashboard itself, the Dashboard icon would link to itself — harmless. \n\nSo: Nav Rail component with 6 variants; inside EACH variant, icon nodes carry reactions → destination frames. Since icons are shared across variants (each variant has its own icon children), set reactions in all 6 variants (or just set on each icon in each variant — 36 reactions). Fine.\n\nAlso 01_Dashboard message rows → 06_Messages: set reactions on those rows in the dashboard frame directly.\n\nNow, how do interactions inside components work for prototyping? A CLICK reaction on a node inside a component triggers navigation when clicked in prototype — yes, standard.\n\nTop Bar: component, instance on all screens. No interactions needed (search/notifications are entry-only).\n\nLet me also confirm: creating instances of components that live on other pages is fine.\n\n**Detailed layout specs:**\n\nCommon:\n- Screen frame: 1440×1024, fill bg/app #F4F5F9, name exact.\n- Top Bar: x=88, y=0, w=1352, h=80, white surface, bottom border #E8E9F0. Content: search field (x=24 inside, w=420, h=44, radius 12? pill), bg #F4F5F9, icon 20, text \"Search anything...\" #8C8C9E. Right side: bell button 40×40 (Icon_Bell) with badge (16 circle brand? badge count 2 — badge red #EB5757 or brand; use brand #5051F9 w/ white text \"2\", positioned top-right), avatar 40 circle, Icon_Chevron_Down 16.\n  Hmm \"notifications (Icon_Bell) with count 2\" —